/**
@page development Developer manual

@brief Working on @c libbotanik itself - the mechanisms, and why they are the way
       they are.

This part is for changing the library, not using it. Each page takes one mechanism
and explains how it actually works; read @ref dev_architecture first, then whichever
subsystem you are touching.

@section dev_metrics At a glance

Regenerated on every docs build by `scripts/code_charts.py`, broken down by code area
(the folder under `src/`). The first sizes each area; the second shows where recent
work has gone.

@htmlonly
<div class="code-charts">
  <figure>
    <img src="code_loc.svg" alt="Lines of code by area"/>
    <figcaption>Lines of code by area (code lines only, via cloc)</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure>
    <img src="code_churn.svg" alt="Churn by area"/>
    <figcaption>Churn by area over the last 12 months: lines changed and commits touched</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>
@endhtmlonly

@subsection dev_metrics_coupling Which way the dependencies point

Every cross-module `#include`, with the modules on both axes ordered bottom-of-stack
to top (`scripts/coupling_matrix.py`). A cell **left of the diagonal** is a module
depending on something beneath it: the layering working. A cell **right of the
diagonal** points back up the stack, and is a layering violation.

Only header (`.h` -> `.h`) edges are shown, because only those are contagious - an
include in a `.cpp` is private to one translation unit, while one in a header lands
in every file that transitively includes it, and is what fuses modules into
dependency cycles. The config headers are held out: every module includes them, so
they would render as a solid column and say nothing.

@htmlonly
<div class="code-charts">
  <figure>
    <img src="coupling.svg" alt="Module dependency matrix"/>
    <figcaption>Cross-module includes; red cells point up the stack</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>
@endhtmlonly

**There are no red cells, and the module graph is acyclic.** That is the point of
keeping this chart where you can see it: the baseline is zero, so the first one to
appear stands out. A baseline of "four known-good exceptions" does not work - the
fifth blends in.

Getting there took no new abstractions, because the last few upward edges were
misplacements rather than couplings:

- `Console` holds `Pages::Page*` and calls it only from `console.cpp`, so the page
  interface is **forward-declared** in `console.h`. A page multiplexer knowing the
  page interface is fine; all of `pages/` landing in everything that draws is not.
- `library_vars.h` - the manifest of rows the library splices into a site's table -
  used to live in `vars/`, which put the variable model above the subsystems that
  contribute to it. It is macro-only and site-facing, so it sits beside the umbrella
  instead. Nothing in `vars/` ever compiled against it.
- `ShellPage` owning a `Shell::CommandShell` was never a violation: `shell/` includes
  nothing from `pages/` (that is what `Shell::ShellWriter` is for), so the consistent
  order is console, then shell, then pages.

What was NOT done is worth recording too. A `PageHost` interface for the console, or
a pimpl'd `ShellPage`, would each buy a green cell at the price of a vtable or an
allocation on a path that does not want one. An indirection added to fix a picture
is not an improvement.

Run `python3 scripts/coupling_matrix.py --text` for the current state; it prints the
exact files behind any upward edge, and says so plainly when there are none. A NEW
red cell is a layering regression, and wants either a fix or a line here saying why
it earns its place.

@section dev_core Core

- @subpage dev_architecture - the layers, the subsystem map, and the per-tick loop
  order.
- @subpage dev_concurrency - one cooperative loop, the async boundaries, and why
  there is no `std::atomic`.
- @subpage dev_scheduler - the fixed-period driver, overrun handling, and the
  self-instrumented metrics.
- @subpage dev_scan - the scan cycle: rate against phase, what a program is
- @subpage dev_modbus - the field bus and the plant network: planning, framing,
  the register map
  guaranteed to see, and where the field bus commits and collects.
- @subpage dev_vars - the variable model: descriptors, value slots, the sink, and
  where overrides are applied.

@section dev_console Console

The console is the subsystem with the most non-obvious machinery, because it drives
a 4800-baud link and every byte is budgeted.

- @subpage dev_input - raw bytes to input events to the active page.
- @subpage dev_pages - the page interface, the render contract, and `Scrollback`.
- @subpage dev_screen - **the TX path.** The screen back-buffer: how a whole
  screen's worth of intent becomes the minimal paced ANSI on a slow link.
- @subpage dev_editor - **editor reconciliation.** The one renderer that still
  reasons about the screen itself, and why it earns the exception.

@section dev_services Services

- @subpage dev_logging - a log entry from call site through rate limiting to every
  sink.
- @subpage dev_health - the power-on self-test, the platform alarms, and the
  watchdog verdict.
- @subpage dev_network - the optional config-driven network stack.

@section dev_platform Platform

- @subpage dev_build - the two build targets, the build options, and the test
  suites.
- @subpage dev_porting - what a third target needs: one new HAL implementation.
- @subpage testing - the test suites in detail.
*/
